<code>assert \forall String prefix; this.startsWith(prefix) && prefix.length() <= word.length() && word.startsWith(prefix) => !this.keysWithPrefix(prefix).contains(word);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall String p; this.startsWith(p) && this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word) => !this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word);</code>

However, the above statement is incorrect because it doesn't properly reflect the condition that the removed word should no longer be found in the results of `keysWithPrefix` for any of its prefixes. Instead, we need to assert that the word itself is no longer in the trie and that none of the prefixes of the word have the word in their results from `keysWithPrefix`.

Let's refine it:

<code>assert !this.contains(word) && \forall String p; word.startsWith(p) => !this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word);</code>

This ensures that the word is no longer in the trie and that none of its prefixes return the word in their list of completions. However, the correct and more precise way to express it would be:

<code>assert !this.contains(word) && \forall String p; word.startsWith(p) => !this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word);</code>

But since `this.contains(word)` already checks if the word is in the trie, we can simplify it to just ensuring that the word is not found in any of its prefixes' completions:

<code>assert \forall String p; word.startsWith(p) => !this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word);</code>

So the final assert statement is:

<code>assert \forall String p; word.startsWith(p) => !this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word);</code>
