<code>assert \forall String prefix; this.startsWith(prefix) && this.keysWithPrefix(prefix).contains(word) => !this.keysWithPrefix(prefix).contains(word);</code>

However, this assertion is not quite correct because it doesn't properly capture the state change due to the removal of the word. A more accurate assertion would be:

<code>assert \forall String prefix; this.startsWith(prefix) && \old(this.keysWithPrefix(prefix)).contains(word) => !this.keysWithPrefix(prefix).contains(word);</code>

This ensures that for any prefix, if the word was previously a key with that prefix, it is no longer a key with that prefix after the removal. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert \forall String prefix; this.startsWith(prefix) && \old(this.keysWithPrefix(prefix)).contains(word) => !this.keysWithPrefix(prefix).contains(word);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall String p; this.startsWith(p) && this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word) => !this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word);</code>

However, the above assertion is not quite correct because it doesn't properly capture the fact that the removed word should no longer be found, but other words with the same prefix might still exist. Let's refine it:

<code>assert !this.contains(word) && (\forall String p; this.startsWith(p) && \old(this.keysWithPrefix(p)).contains(word) => !this.keysWithPrefix(p).contains(word));</code>

This asserts that the word is no longer contained in the trie and that none of the prefixes that previously had the word as a completion will have it anymore after the removal.
